WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:06.040
You just had to look
at a smallpox sufferer

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.680
to be horrified.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:11.920
It was very much a lottery ticket,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.080
which most people didn't want to buy.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:17.240
The eradication of smallpox
is one of the most

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.080
significant events
in the 20th Century.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.680
For me it would be up there
with the moon landing.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.360
You certainly couldn't miss a
patient who had got smallpox.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.080
You had these blisters,
these lesions all over your skin.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.560
All these pustules
were filled with virus.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.600
India, as in the Mughal Empire,

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.600
understood the value of variolation

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:37.120
because it saw smallpox
as a threat to military power.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.280
But the problem was that
it was as risky as it sounds -

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.880
you're basically trying to stop
a deadly disease by giving somebody

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.120
a mild case
of the same deadly disease.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.320
Variolation could lead
to uncontrolled epidemics.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:58.240
People knew that this was something

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.200
that their children would catch

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:02.840
and they might survive
or they might die from it

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:04.600
and so it was part
of the cycle of life.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:12.920
When Jenner inoculated the arm of James Phipps,

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:14.320
a young boy of eight,

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.700
with the contents of a cowpox bled from a dairy maid,

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.480
it became possible for the first time to protect human beings artificially

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.400
against pathogenic organisms.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.320
This really tipped the table in
favour of prevention and eradication.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.120
To people in the 1700s,
this was totally mind-blowing.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.400
Jenner's idea was a game changer.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.120
What happens in 1948
after the Second World War,

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.080
the horrors of the Second World War,

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.880
is that the world agrees that
a new world order was needed.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:10.120
What a crazy idea it would be
to say that you're going to institute

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.200
vaccination throughout
the entire world.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.240
The 11th World Health Assembly

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:22.880
approved a resolution in 1958 calling for worldwide smallpox eradication.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.080
The WHO galvanised enthusiasm,
they standardised the vaccine,

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:30.080
obtained the resources
that they needed internationally.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.520
They really deserve
tremendous credit for that.

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:37.000
People running the programme

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.880
were going from
door to door with pictures

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:42.520
of a child with smallpox
and asking people in the community

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.760
if they knew anyone
who had this disease.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.360
You're tracking, testing, isolating.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.520
It's a strategy which
many people have said

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.600
will be the only way we can
keep on top of Covid-19.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:59.720
May, 1980.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:03.120
Two men affix their signatures to an historic document.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:15.680
National and local health workers
played an immense role

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.640
in the eradication of smallpox,

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:19.840
whether it's in Africa or in Asia -

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.520
often selflessly, because
a disease like smallpox was

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:27.720
as threatening to them as Covid-19
is to today's health workers.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:33.480
It's interesting that we're often so
eager to commemorate success in wars,

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:36.040
but that much less is done
to celebrate success

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:38.160
in the control of disease.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:40.960
The eradication of smallpox
is one of the most

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:42.880
significant events
in the 20th Century.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.960
For me it would be up there
with the moon landing.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.280
We've eradicated smallpox.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.280
And because we've eradicated smallpox

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.480
we know that we can eradicate
other human diseases.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.000
If we all work together
to tackle the disease,

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.400
to stop it spreading,
to protect ourselves,

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.960
there is no reason why
we can't stop another pandemic

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:02.720
like we stopped smallpox.

